# `match`, and `given … otherwise`

HQL branches on values, never on control flow. There are two forms, both core
operators and both expressions: `match` takes a value apart by its cases, and
`given … otherwise` is the short form for a single condition.

Neither borrows `if`/`else`. Those words belong to statement-oriented control
flow, and HQL has no statements to steer — a branch is a value like any other.
Keeping them unreserved also leaves them free for functions and future flow
constructs.

## `match`

`match` takes a value and one arm per case. The first arm whose pattern fits
produces the result:

```hql
match c.metadata.genre {
    some(g) => g,
    none    => "unshelved",
}
```

It is how a sum type is consumed: [[option-type|Option]] by `some(v)` and
`none`, and, once `Result` is built in, `Result` by `ok(v)` and `err(e)`. A pattern may also be a literal or
`_`, which fits anything:

```hql
match parse_pages(c) {
    ok(n)  => n,
    err(_) => 0,
}

match c.kind {
    "ConceptCard"  => "node",
    "RelationCard" => "edge",
}
```

**`match` is exhaustive.** Every case of the type must be covered, or the
checker rejects the match. A `match` over a `String` or an `Int` therefore needs
`_`; one over an `Option` needs both `some` and `none`. This is what makes
absence and failure safe to consume: a forgotten `none` is a type error, not a
silent `false`.

All arms produce one type, the common type of their results, and that is the
type of the `match`.

### Guards

An arm can narrow its pattern with `given`, the same word as the short form:

```hql
match c.metadata.pages {
    some(n) given n > 400 => "long",
    some(_)               => "short",
    none                  => "unknown",
}
```

A guarded arm does not count toward exhaustiveness, because its condition may
fail; an unguarded arm for the same case must follow it.

## `given … otherwise`

The short form reads as a sentence — this value, given a condition, otherwise
that one:

```hql
"adult" given p.age >= 18 otherwise "minor"
```

It is exactly a two-arm `match` on a `Bool`, and the condition must be a
`Bool`: an `Option<Bool>` is refused until it says what absence means, with
`unwrap_or`.

**Without `otherwise`, the result is an `Option`.** A value given a condition,
and nothing otherwise, is a value that may be absent:

```hql
"long" given n > 400               // Option<String>
"long" given n > 400 otherwise ""  // String
```

So `otherwise` is the same step as `unwrap_or` on that `Option`, and the short
form composes with the rest of the option vocabulary instead of standing beside
it:

```hql
cards | map(c => c.name given c.metadata.genre == "novel")   // List<Option<String>>
```

## Absence is consumed explicitly

Today absence propagates implicitly through fields and steps — a stopgap the
[[option-type]] card records. With `match`, `given` and the `Option`
operations, every place absence is consumed can say what it means there. Whether
the implicit lifting survives once they exist is decided by what the corpus
still needs it for.

Implemented: value arms over `Option`, `Bool`, `Int`, `Float` and `String`,
`_` and binding names, guards, exhaustiveness, and `given … otherwise` with and
without `otherwise`. `Result` arms wait for `Result`, and the type arm of
[[type-interpretation]] is separate work. A string or number pattern also fits
a `Data` leaf with that value. See [[option-type]] for absence,
[optional fields and optional values](../../../models/behavior/options-in-types-and-fields.md)
for `Option` and `Result` and their operations, and
[[error-reporting]] for the errors that are not values at all.
